  "question": {
    "text": "If \\(f(x)=\\frac{1-x}{1+x}\\) the domain of \\(f^{-1}(x)\\) is"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "\\(\\mathrm{R}\\)",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "\\(\\mathrm{R}-\\{-1\\}\\)",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "\\((-\\infty,-1)\\)",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "\\((-1, \\infty)\\)",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "B"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "Let \\(f(x)=y\\). Then \\(\\frac{1-x}{1+x}=y\\)\n\\(\\Rightarrow x=\\frac{1-y}{1+y} \\Rightarrow f^{-1}(y)=\\frac{1-y}{1+y}\\)\nThus, \\(f^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1-x}{1+x}\\)\nClearly, \\(\\mathrm{f}^{-1}(\\mathrm{x})\\) is defined for \\(1+\\mathrm{x} \\neq 0\\).\nHence, domain of \\(\\mathrm{f}^{-1}(\\mathrm{x})\\) is \\(\\mathrm{R}-\\{-1\\}\\)"
  }
}